# =============================================================================
# SUNDAY RELAY — ENVIRONMENT CONTRACT (example values only)
# =============================================================================
# Copy to `.env` and fill in locally. `.env` and `.env.*` are gitignored;
# `.env.example` is the only one tracked and it carries NO real value, ever.
#
# Relay is an independent product with its own environment scope. No Alcatraz
# secret is copied here — see docs/REPOSITORY_GOVERNANCE.md §10.
#
# Nothing in the default test, build, demo or CI path reads any of these.
# Every variable below only widens what a LOCAL, explicitly-confirmed run may
# do; leaving the file untouched keeps Relay fully offline.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RELAY BRIDGE (relay-bridge/) — the browser surface's only backend
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The bridge runs SERVER-SIDE on your machine. It holds provider auth locally;
# no provider key is ever read in the browser bundle or committed here.

# Port the bridge listens on. Vite proxies /relay-api here (vite.config.mts).
RELAY_BRIDGE_PORT=8790

# Browser origin allowed to call the bridge. Leave empty for the Vite default.
ALLOWED_FRONTEND_ORIGIN=

# REQUIRED (=1) to permit a REAL Claude Code run. The browser equivalent of the
# CLI's --confirm-live. Never inferred; empty means no live run is possible.
RELAY_BRIDGE_CONFIRM_LIVE=

# =1 runs the whole pipeline against the repository's offline fake Claude
# executable: no model call, no spend. Proves the bridge end to end without keys.
RELAY_BRIDGE_FAKE_CLAUDE=

# Depth requested from the architect backend: fast | balanced | deep.
RELAY_BRIDGE_SUNDAY_MODE=balanced

# THE LOOP ENGINE SWITCH, and the only one. Exactly =1 enables it; unset,
# empty, "true", "yes" and " 1" are all OFF. Default off: every Loop route
# answers loop_engine_disabled without it, and it guards work that spends.
RELAY_LOOP_ENGINE_ENABLED=

# Cron Loops, gated BEHIND the engine switch above. Exactly =1, same rule.
# What it enables is POST /relay-api/cron/tick — an operator-authenticated
# manual tick that creates scheduled Loop run records. It does NOT start a
# scheduler: nothing calls the tick on a timer, and a scheduled run is
# created and never advanced. See docs/relay/CRON_LOOPS.md.
RELAY_LOOP_CRON_ENABLED=

# CROSS-PRODUCT INTEGRATION, and the only one Relay has. The bridge's architect
# leg can route a brief through a running Sunday Alcatraz backend, addressed by
# URL only — no Alcatraz code is imported and no Alcatraz secret is read.
# Leave empty unless you are deliberately running that integration locally.
# Tracked as an open item in the integration PR; governance §11 permits a typed
# integration but not an implementation dependency.
FUSION_BASE_URL=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PROMPT ARCHITECT (server-side only)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Live mode is EXPLICIT: a key alone never enables a paid call.

# Which Prompt Architect holds the role, and therefore whether this mission
# reviews at all.
#   live    the paid ChatGPT architect. A Reviewer becomes REQUIRED.
#   fusion  the Sunday Alcatraz development architect. No spend, and the
#           mission does not dispatch a Reviewer.
#   empty   BLOCKED — not "offline". No mission can start at all.
#
# HOSTED SHAPES, AND WHAT EACH ALSO NEEDS:
#   fusion + RELAY_BRIDGE_FAKE_CLAUDE=1, both role selectors unset — the
#     keyless offline pipeline, no spend. ALSO needs a reachable Sunday
#     Alcatraz at FUSION_BASE_URL: `fusion` is an HTTP architect, and a
#     production bridge whose FUSION_BASE_URL is still the localhost default
#     fails at `architect_unavailable` before any role is dispatched.
#   fusion + RELAY_ROLE_CODING_AGENT=claude_agent_sdk_hosted — a real, hosted,
#     API-billed coding run with no Reviewer (the development architect
#     dispatches none). Same FUSION_BASE_URL requirement.
# A LIVE hosted three-role mission still cannot run at all: the only hosted
# Reviewer occupant is not dispatchable, because the mission's reviewer leg
# spawns a local process and does not use the remote transport.
#
# SO, TRUTHFULLY, ON RAILWAY AS CONFIGURED TODAY: no shape completes a mission.
# The live architect is configured but a live mission needs a Reviewer; the
# development architect needs a Fusion host that production does not point at.
RELAY_PROMPT_ARCHITECT_MODE=

# Server-only. NEVER a VITE_ variable, never in the browser bundle.
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# e.g. a chat-completions model your account can reach.
OPENAI_PROMPT_ARCHITECT_MODEL=

# Output ceiling and timeout for that single call.
RELAY_PROMPT_ARCHITECT_MAX_TOKENS=1200
RELAY_PROMPT_ARCHITECT_TIMEOUT_MS=90000


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REVIEWER RUNTIME (server-side only)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The reviewer's provider, model and billing mode are separate facts and must
# each be reported truthfully. Do not infer billing from the executable name.

RELAY_HERMES_EXECUTABLE=hermes
RELAY_HERMES_MODEL=
RELAY_HERMES_PROVIDER=
# api_billed | subscription | portal | local | unknown
RELAY_HERMES_BILLING_MODE=
RELAY_HERMES_TIMEOUT_MS=180000
RELAY_HERMES_MAX_OUTPUT=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BROWSER BUILD FLAGS — non-secret URLs and switches ONLY
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Anything prefixed VITE_ is INLINED INTO THE PUBLIC BUNDLE. Never put a
# provider key, token, or credential in one. Typed in src/relay/vite-env.d.ts
# and read in src/relay/ui/app/store.ts.

# =1 selects the LIVE bridge adapter instead of the offline demo adapter.
VITE_RELAY_LIVE=

# Explicit bridge base URL. Empty = same-origin /relay-api via the Vite proxy.
VITE_RELAY_BRIDGE_URL=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ROLE SLOTS — which occupant holds each permanent role. Server-only.
#
# Relay's three roles are fixed; who stands in them is not. These SELECT an
# occupant. The RELAY_HERMES_* names below CONFIGURE the transport a selected
# reviewer needs — two different jobs, deliberately two different variables,
# because one variable with two readers had no value that satisfied both.
#
# A HOSTED deployment must name every role it will dispatch. It gets no
# defaults: guessing which agent holds a role on a machine the operator cannot
# see is the bug the transport seam was built to remove.
#
# The Prompt Architect is selected by RELAY_PROMPT_ARCHITECT_MODE (above), and
# on the development path (`fusion`) the Reviewer is not dispatched at all, so
# it need not be named.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Which Coding Agent occupant holds the role. Registered ids:
#   claude_code_local        the installed Claude Code CLI. Founder machine ONLY
#                            — a container will never have it.
#   claude_agent_sdk_hosted  the Claude Agent SDK, API-billed, runs anywhere.
#                            Needs ANTHROPIC_API_KEY and RELAY_HOSTED_CODING_MODEL.
#   claude_code_fake         the keyless offline pipeline. Selected ONLY by
#                            RELAY_BRIDGE_FAKE_CLAUDE=1. Naming it here without
#                            that variable is refused: the real Claude Code CLI
#                            would run and be recorded as having spent nothing.
RELAY_ROLE_CODING_AGENT=

# Which Reviewer occupant holds the role. Registered ids:
#   hermes_local             a Hermes process this machine spawns. Founder
#                            machine ONLY.
#   hermes_remote_service    the dedicated Hermes service over authenticated
#                            HTTP. Runs anywhere, and NOT YET DISPATCHABLE by
#                            the mission pipeline — the mission's reviewer leg
#                            still spawns locally. It IS used by the
#                            /reviewer/* routes.
RELAY_ROLE_REVIEWER=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HERMES REVIEWER — server-only. None of these may ever use a VITE_ name.
#
# Installing Hermes on an operator laptop does NOT make a hosted Relay Bridge
# connected: readiness runs inside whichever container answers. The dedicated
# Hermes Reviewer service is what a hosted deployment reaches through the
# /reviewer/* routes. Repository artifacts existing is not deployment.
#
# TRUTHFULLY: a hosted deployment cannot yet run the MISSION's reviewer leg by
# any configuration. `runHermesReview` spawns a local process and does not
# consult the transport below, so `hermes_remote_service` binds and is then
# refused as not dispatchable. Deploying the service enables the reviewer
# ROUTES; it does not yet enable a hosted three-role mission.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# --- Bridge, LOCAL mode (development on a machine that really has Hermes) ---
# The bridge spawns Hermes itself, using RELAY_HERMES_EXECUTABLE,
# RELAY_HERMES_PROVIDER and RELAY_HERMES_MODEL — each declared exactly ONCE, in
# REVIEWER RUNTIME above, and deliberately not repeated here. A .env is read
# last-wins, so a second definition silently overrides the first and the
# operator ends up debugging a value they cannot see.
#
# Empty is the honest default: production fails closed on an unset mode, and
# off production it means local. Relay never guesses which one you meant.
RELAY_HERMES_MODE=

# Provider credentials — server-only, and empty here by contract. Exactly one
# of these is required, chosen by RELAY_HERMES_PROVIDER and never inferred from
# whichever one happens to be set.
#
# The PROVIDER decides which variable carries the credential, and a caller
# cannot name it. An xai run receives XAI_API_KEY / XAI_BASE_URL and no
# Anthropic variable; an anthropic run receives ANTHROPIC_API_KEY /
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL and no xAI variable. A credential never travels under
# another vendor's name.
XAI_API_KEY=
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Optional per-provider base URLs. Left unset, each provider's default is used.
# XAI_BASE_URL=
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=

# Run limits are CLAMPED by the service, which owns the credential and pays for
# every run: timeoutMs <= 600000, maxOutputBytes <= 1048576,
# maxPromptBytes <= 262144, and maxTurns must be exactly 1 because the adapter
# is one-shot. A caller may ask for less; asking for more is clamped, not
# honoured. These are service constants, not environment variables.

# --- Bridge, REMOTE mode (production) ---
# The bridge is a CLIENT and never spawns anything. It must NOT receive
# XAI_API_KEY or ANTHROPIC_API_KEY in this mode — provider keys belong only to
# the Hermes Reviewer service.
# RELAY_HERMES_MODE=remote
# RELAY_HERMES_SERVICE_TOKEN=
#
# THE SCHEME IS PART OF THE ORIGIN, SO GET IT RIGHT ONCE.
# Railway private networking speaks PLAIN HTTP over an internal IPv6 name and
# terminates no TLS, so a private-domain deployment is `http://…`:
# RELAY_HERMES_SERVICE_URL=http://<service>.railway.internal:8080
# RELAY_HERMES_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=http://<service>.railway.internal:8080
#
# A service reached over the public internet is `https://…` and both lines
# change together:
# RELAY_HERMES_SERVICE_URL=https://<public-service-domain>
# RELAY_HERMES_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=https://<public-service-domain>
#
# The two must agree exactly. `https://host` and `http://host` are DIFFERENT
# origins and the gate refuses the mismatch — which is the intended behaviour
# and a confusing thirty minutes if the example above had guessed for you.
#
# RELAY_HERMES_TRUSTED_ORIGINS is REQUIRED in production and the bridge fails
# closed without it: the service URL's origin must appear here EXACTLY, because
# this is the list of places the bridge is willing to send its bearer token.
#   - exact origins only — scheme, host and port, e.g. https://host:8443
#   - no wildcards, and no bare hosts: a host without a scheme would silently
#     admit plaintext http
#   - no suffix matching: evil-relay.internal ends with relay.internal
# RELAY_HERMES_SERVICE_URL itself must carry no query and no fragment. Endpoint
# paths are appended to it, so either one would silently send every call to a
# different path. Redirects are refused rather than followed, so the token
# never reaches an origin that is not on this list.
#
# Outside production the policy is loopback-only unless an origin is listed
# here, so a real staging host stays reachable by naming it.

# With RELAY_HERMES_MODE unset, production fails closed rather than probing the
# bridge's own container. Off production it defaults to local.

# --- The dedicated Hermes Reviewer service ---
# RELAY_HERMES_SERVICE_TOKEN=          # shared with the bridge; required to start
# RELAY_HERMES_EXECUTABLE=/path/to/hermes
# RELAY_HERMES_PROVIDER=xai            # or anthropic — NEVER inferred from which key is set
# RELAY_HERMES_MODEL=grok-4            # exact model id; Relay chooses none
# XAI_API_KEY=                         # only when provider is xai
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=                   # only when provider is anthropic
# HOST=0.0.0.0
# PORT=                                # injected by the platform

# The browser never receives a provider key or the service token. There is
# deliberately no VITE_XAI_API_KEY, VITE_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or
# VITE_RELAY_HERMES_SERVICE_TOKEN, and tests assert their absence.
